{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by the political instability of the Crusader states. He inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Because of his young age a regency was established with his mother Hodierna and his grandfather Count Joscelin II of Edessa overseeing his rule. During this time Tripoli faced constant threats from Muslim forces and internal rivalries among the nobility. Raymond III was known to be a cautious and calculating leader who preferred diplomacy over direct confrontation.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a key player in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was married to Eschiva of Bures and later to Imelda of Lusignan. His marriage alliances strengthened his position and brought him closer to other noble families in the region. He served as regent of Jerusalem twice during the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was often at odds with other powerful nobles such as Guy of Lusignan and the Knights Templar. He was accused of being too willing to negotiate with Muslim leaders including Saladin. His diplomatic efforts were viewed by some as cowardly but by others as pragmatic given the fragile state of the Crusader territories.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He opposed Guy of Lusignan’s decision to march toward Tiberias and instead advocated for a defensive strategy. His warnings were ignored and the Crusader army was decisively defeated. Raymond survived the battle and retreated to Tripoli. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of an era of cautious leadership in the Crusader states. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a complex figure whose diplomacy and reluctance to engage in reckless warfare reflected the difficult realities of maintaining Christian rule in a hostile environment. His legacy is debated among historians with some seeing him as a realist and others as a hesitant leader who failed to act decisively in critical moments.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0013335392577573657}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.2305197237292305e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.01815054751932621}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.0251946150674485e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.011605959385633469}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.006394876167178154}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.08049094676971436}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.775113539944869e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009245174005627632}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0002057340752799064}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.992108420585282e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.31327706575393677}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0012085284106433392}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -3.4570634852570947e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.3841574147809297e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token